Get the edition with all DLC. This game is really good because:
- one unit per tile means that more is needed to win a war than having a larger army; this also means you can defeat vastly more numerous and powerful armies if they are dumb (i.e. played by AI), which is exactly what will happen on high difficulty;
- very high importance of terrain and resources make every game different and create a lot of tactical and strategic decisions, in part because of the first point;
- you will generally encounter few "oneshots": in most cases you have a chance to react before your unit dies or your city is captured;
- the AI is at least able to build units, send them your way and have them attack, which is commonly enough to kill you with the insane bonuses it gets;
- the terrain looks very good and it's easy to tell what is what (mostly where the hills are).
This list was easy to compile because both of the more recent Civ games failed at more than one of the above. I played much more than is listed in Steam profile and quit simply because it was too much of one game, even though it's my favorite game of all time.

