I’m reviewing this game based on my SINGLE PLAYER experience. I cannot comment on the multiplayer aspect since I have never played it.
Battlefleet Gothic: Armada is a real-time strategy (RTS) game released in 2016. It was developed by Tindalos Interactive and published by Focus Home Interactive. This title is based on the Warhammer 40,000 tabletop game called “Battlefleet Gothic” by Games Workshop.
The plot revolves around a retelling of the story of the 12th Black Crusade by Abaddon the Despoiler. The story places you in the shoes of Captain Spire in his great struggle to defend the Gothic Sector against the enemies of mankind, whether they be Ork, Eldar, Traitors, or Chaos. Command the great ships of the Battlefleet Gothic, advance through the ranks, push back the Chaos invasion, and most importantly, keep your faith in the Emperor.
+ Very good graphics and art style;
+ Good story with a lengthy game time;
+ Excellent voice acting and music;
+ Great (and lore accurate) ship designs;
+ Good selection of ship upgrades that opens up a variety of builds;
+ Good micromanagement options (ex. Ship orientation, special weapons, boarding actions, ramming speed, focus fire, emergency repairs, etc.);
+ Fairly easy to learn game mechanics;
+ The game’s ending is based on your performance (good or bad ending);
+ Captures the Warhammer 40k atmosphere perfectly;
+ Some of the best Warhammer 40k cutscenes I have ever seen; and
+ A limited progression system is present for Skirmish mode (admiral level up and ship level up)
- Large fleets battles are not possible in the campaign due to the very restrictive point system;
- Leveling up your Admiral ranking does not give a lot of bonuses;
- Other than color scheme, you can’t customize the look of your ships – I would really like to change the Captain portraits;
- Some frustrating missions involving running around the battle map chasing objectives;
- Stupid and suicidal friendly AI; and
- Repetitive missions.
+/- Only has the Imperial Campaign (would have liked campaigns for the different factions with an overlapping story, like the Dawn of War series);
+/- Could use more stuff to do when not in battle other than upgrading ships; and
+/- Unskippable intro cutscene for each battle.
game that captures the Warhammer 40k universe perfectly. It delivers fun gameplay that scratches an itch for space tactical ship battles, it has a good story and allows enough customization options to tailor your fleet to a preferred play style. It’s far from perfect but it has a lot to offer for both 40k and RTS fans alike.
The game is worth $39.99 or your regional equivalent. Although I recommend this game, I feel that the price is a bit high for the content that it delivers. If you’re interested in this game, you should pick it up during a sale.
This is not a 4X game like Sins of a Solar Empire, rather, Battlefleet Gothic: Armada is similar to the space battles of Star Wars: Empire at War.
